vendor\league\plates\doc\content\templates\data.md:
  31  
  32: Template data is available as locally scoped variables at the time of rendering. Continuing with the example above, here is how you would [escape]({{< relref "templates/escaping.md" >}}) and output the "name" value in a template:
  33  
  34  ~~~ php
  35: <p>Hello <?=$this->e($name)?></p>
  36  ~~~
  37  
  38: <p class="message-notice">Prior to Plates 3.0, variables were accessed using the <code>$this</code> pseudo-variable. This is no longer possible. Use the locally scoped variables instead.</p>
  39  

vendor\league\plates\doc\content\templates\escaping.md:
  13  ~~~ php
  14: <h1>Hello, <?=$this->escape($name)?></h1>
  15  
  16: <!-- Using the alternative, shorthand function -->
  17: <h1>Hello, <?=$this->e($name)?></h1>
  18  ~~~

  21  
  22: The escape functions also support [batch]({{< relref "templates/functions.md#batch-function-calls" >}}) function calls, which allow you to apply multiple functions, including native PHP functions, to a variable at one time.
  23  
  24  ~~~ php
  25: <p>Welcome <?=$this->e($name, 'strip_tags|strtoupper')?></p>
  26  ~~~

  29  
  30: <p class="message-notice">It's VERY important to always double quote HTML attributes that contain escaped variables, otherwise your template will still be open to injection attacks.</p>
  31  

  34  ~~~ php
  35: <!-- Good -->
  36: <img src="portrait.jpg" alt="<?=$this->e($name)?>">
  37  
  38: <!-- BAD -->
  39: <img src="portrait.jpg" alt='<?=$this->e($name)?>'>
  40  
  41: <!-- BAD -->
  42: <img src="portrait.jpg" alt=<?=$this->e($name)?>>
  43  ~~~

vendor\league\plates\doc\content\templates\functions.md:
  11  ~~~ php
  12: <p>Hello, <?=$this->escape($name)?></p>
  13  ~~~

  17  
  18: In addition to the functions included with Plates, it's also possible to add [one-off functions]({{< relref "engine/functions.md" >}}), or even groups of functions, known as [extensions]({{< relref "engine/extensions.md" >}}).
  19  

  24  ~~~ php
  25: <!-- Example without using batch -->
  26: <p>Welcome <?=$this->escape(strtoupper(strip_tags($name)))?></p>
  27  
  28: <!-- Example using batch -->
  29: <p>Welcome <?=$this->batch($name, 'strip_tags|strtoupper|escape')?></p>
  30  ~~~
  31  
  32: The [escape]({{< relref "templates/escaping.md" >}}) functions also support batch function calls.
  33  
  34  ~~~ php
  35: <p>Welcome <?=$this->e($name, 'strip_tags|strtoupper')?></p>
  36  ~~~

  40  ~~~ php
  41: <!-- Will output: JONATHAN -->
  42: <?=$this->batch('Jonathan', 'escape|strtolower|strtoupper')?>
  43  
  44: <!-- Will output: jonathan -->
  45: <?=$this->batch('Jonathan', 'escape|strtoupper|strtolower')?>
  46  ~~~

vendor\league\plates\doc\content\templates\inheritance.md:
   8  
   9: By combining [layouts]({{< relref "templates/layouts.md" >}}) and [sections]({{< relref "templates/sections.md" >}}), Plates allows you to "build up" your pages using predefined sections. This is best understand using an example:
  10  

  12  
  13: The following example illustrates a pretty standard website. Start by creating a site template, which includes your header and footer as well as any predefined content [sections]({{< relref "templates/sections.md" >}}). Notice how Plates makes it possible to even set default section content, in the event that a page doesn't define it.
  14  
  15: {{< code-filename template.php >}}
  16  
  17  ~~~ php
  18: <html>
  19: <head>
  20:     <title><?=$this->e($title)?></title>
  21: </head>
  22: <body>
  23  
  24: <img src="logo.png">
  25  
  26: <div id="page">
  27:     <?=$this->section('page')?>
  28: </div>
  29  
  30: <div id="sidebar">
  31:     <?php if ($this->section('sidebar')): ?>
  32:         <?=$this->section('sidebar')?>
  33:     <?php else: ?>
  34:         <?=$this->fetch('default-sidebar')?>
  35:     <?php endif ?>
  36: </div>
  37  
  38: </body>
  39: </html>
  40  ~~~
  41  
  42: With the template defined, any page can now "implement" this [layout]({{< relref "templates/layouts.md" >}}). Notice how each section of content is defined between the `start()` and `end()` functions.
  43  
  44: {{< code-filename profile.php >}}
  45  
  46  ~~~ php
  47: <?php $this->layout('template', ['title' => 'User Profile']) ?>
  48  
  49: <?php $this->start('page') ?>
  50:     <h1>Welcome!</h1>
  51:     <p>Hello <?=$this->e($name)?></p>
  52: <?php $this->stop() ?>
  53  
  54: <?php $this->start('sidebar') ?>
  55:     <ul>
  56:         <li><a href="/link">Example Link</a></li>
  57:         <li><a href="/link">Example Link</a></li>
  58:         <li><a href="/link">Example Link</a></li>
  59:         <li><a href="/link">Example Link</a></li>
  60:         <li><a href="/link">Example Link</a></li>
  61:     </ul>
  62: <?php $this->stop() ?>
  63  ~~~

vendor\league\plates\doc\content\templates\layouts.md:
   15  ~~~ php
   16: <?php $this->layout('template') ?>
   17  
   18: <h1>User Profile</h1>
   19: <p>Hello, <?=$this->e($name)?></p>
   20  ~~~
   21  
   22: This function also works with [folders]({{< relref "engine/folders.md" >}}):
   23  
   24  ~~~ php
   25: <?php $this->layout('shared::template') ?>
   26  ~~~

   32  ~~~ php
   33: <?php $this->layout('template', ['title' => 'User Profile']) ?>
   34  ~~~

   37  
   38: To access the rendered template content within the layout, use the `section()` function, passing `'content'` as the section name. This will return all outputted content from the template that hasn't been defined in a [section]({{< relref "templates/sections.md" >}}).
   39  
   40  ~~~ php
   41: <html>
   42: <head>
   43:     <title><?=$this->e($title)?></title>
   44: </head>
   45: <body>
   46  
   47: <?=$this->section('content')?>
   48  
   49: </body>
   50: </html>
   51  ~~~

   58  
   59: {{< code-filename template.php >}}
   60  
   61  ~~~ php
   62: <html>
   63: <head>
   64:     <title><?=$this->e($title)?></title>
   65: </head>
   66: <body>
   67  
   68: <?=$this->section('content')?>
   69  
   70: </body>
   71: </html>
   72  ~~~

   75  
   76: {{< code-filename blog.php >}}
   77  
   78  ~~~ php
   79: <?php $this->layout('template', ['title' => $title]) ?>
   80  
   81: <h1>The Blog</h1>
   82  
   83: <section>
   84:     <article>
   85:         <?=$this->section('content')?>
